# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python source is stored and checked out with LF, on every platform.
#
# `tools/mutate.py` patches source by matching an anchor **byte for byte**, and
# several anchors in `benchmarks/mutations/*.json` span more than one line. Those
# anchors are written with LF, because that is how the repository stores them. A
# default Git-for-Windows clone sets `core.autocrlf=true`, which would hand the
# working copy CRLF -- so a multi-line anchor could not match, the harness would
# refuse with `AnchorMissing`, and the heavy self-sweep would fail on a fresh
# checkout that did nothing wrong. It fails safe rather than miscounting, which is
# the harness working; it is still a tier that cannot run, and PR #5 exists
# precisely because a tier nobody can run reports on nothing.
#
# Narrow on purpose: `*.py`, not `* text=auto`. A blanket normalisation would
# reach every recorded byte in this repository, and several of those are pinned by
# digest.
#
# This rule comes FIRST so the `-text` exceptions below override it -- the last
# matching pattern wins. Eight `.py` files live inside those hash-pinned areas
# (`benchmarks/references/vent-ball-combine/evidence/model.py`, six recorded
# `model.py` inputs under `benchmarks/replays/`, and
# `benchmarks/replays/test_l1_replay.py`). They are recorded content whose bytes
# are digested into a `case.json`, not source anybody edits, and normalising them
# is exactly the `CaseMismatch` this file was written to prevent.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.py text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The vendored benchmark fixture is recorded by SHA-256 and must survive a round
# trip through git unchanged.
#
# `vent_mount.step` is ASCII and arrived with CRLF line endings. Left alone, git
# normalises those to LF on commit and converts back on checkout according to
# whatever `core.autocrlf` says on the machine doing the checkout -- so the file
# a clone produces is not the file whose hash `tools/fixtures.py` records, and
# `resolve()` raises `FixtureMismatch` on a checkout that did nothing wrong. The
# same applies to the evidence beside the reference, which is worth keeping only
# because it is exactly what was produced.
#
# `-text` turns the conversion off entirely: these bytes are stored and restored
# verbatim on every platform.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
benchmarks/fixtures/vent-ball-combine/public/sources/** -text
benchmarks/references/** -text

# The same argument, one tier up. A replay case's `inputs/` and `judgements/` are
# digested into its own `case.json`, and `replay.load()` refuses a case whose
# recorded bytes moved -- so a checkout that normalised a newline would raise
# `CaseMismatch` on a machine that did nothing wrong. `case.json` and
# `expected.json` are not themselves digested, and are covered anyway rather than
# leaving a reader to work out which half of one directory is protected.
benchmarks/replays/** -text
